// Finding sounds and functions
// The ♫ button at the top opens the
// Sounds panel: drum machines,
// synths, instruments and more.
// Tap a sound to hear it, even while
// your music plays.
//
// Try: open Sounds, pick a sound you
// like, and write its name in place
// of cp below. Update.

$: s("bd*2 sd")
$: s("~ cp ~ cp")
// ---
// Drum machines
// A drum machine is a set of drums
// with the usual names. bank() picks
// one: bd, sd, hh then come from it.
//
// Try: in Sounds, open Drum machines
// and swap RolandTR909 for another,
// like RolandTR808 or LinnDrum.
// Update.

$: s("bd hh sd hh")
  .bank("RolandTR909")
// ---
// The reference
// The book button at the top opens
// the Reference: every function,
// what it does, and examples. Search
// by name, then Insert the call or a
// whole example into your code.
//
// Try: search for jux and read about
// it. Then add .jux(rev) at the end
// of the hh line. Update.

$: s("bd sd")
$: s("hh*8").gain(0.5)
// ---
// Suggestions and docs
// While you type a name, suggestions
// show above the keyboard: tap one to
// complete it, or its (i) to read
// about it. The ⋮ menu at the top,
// then Docs at cursor, explains the
// function under the cursor.
//
// Try: on a new line after triangle,
// type .ro and pick room. Then give
// it a number: .room(0.5)

$: s("bd ~ sd ~")
$: note("c3 eb3 g3 bb3")
  .s("triangle")
// ---
// The console
// ⋮ then Console shows what the code
// prints, and its warnings. .log()
// prints every event of a pattern.
//
// Try: open the Console, play, and
// watch the notes go by.
//
// Lesson 4: cycles and tempo.

$: note("c3 e3 g3").s("piano").log()
